-- Q376: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["IFRS requires single-step format while GAAP requires multi-step format for all entities","Both frameworks mandate identical expense classification approaches on the income statement","GAAP requires presentation by nature of expense while IFRS requires presentation by function","IFRS permits classification by either nature or function, while GAAP generally requires functional classification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IAS 1.99, IFRS allows entities to present expenses by nature (e.g., depreciation, salaries) or by function (e.g., cost of sales, administrative). U.S. GAAP, through ASC 220-10-45 and SEC Regulation S-X Rule 5-03, generally requires functional classification for public companies.
Wrong (A): Neither framework mandates single-step or multi-step format; the distinction is in expense classification approach.
Wrong (B): The frameworks differ — IFRS offers flexibility that GAAP does not.
Wrong (C): This reverses the actual treatment — GAAP requires functional classification, not nature.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 376;

-- Q2149: Contingencies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Falcon''s accrual remains at $3,000,000 because the proportionate allocation is fixed at the judgment date","Falcon should reduce its accrual to zero because Delta''s bankruptcy suspends all co-defendants'' obligations","Falcon should increase its accrual to the full $9,000,000 regardless of Summit''s continued solvency","Falcon should increase its accrual to $4,500,000, splitting Delta''s share equally with the remaining solvent co-defendant"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 450-20-25, loss contingency accruals must reflect the best estimate of probable loss. Joint-and-several liability means each defendant can be held liable for the full $9,000,000. With Delta insolvent, Falcon and Summit split Delta''s $3,000,000 share, raising Falcon''s estimate to $4,500,000.
Wrong (A): Contingency estimates must be updated when co-defendant solvency changes materially per ASC 450.
Wrong (B): Delta''s bankruptcy stay does not extinguish other defendants'' joint-and-several obligations.
Wrong (C): While $9,000,000 is the theoretical maximum, the best estimate considers Summit''s continuing solvency.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2149;

-- Q10054: Public Company Reporting [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Diluted EPS decreases because 5,000 incremental shares are added, though the specific method is not specified","Diluted EPS is unaffected because out-of-the-money written put options are excluded from the calculation","Diluted EPS decreases because the reverse treasury stock method adds 5,000 incremental shares to the denominator","Diluted EPS increases because the company receives net cash inflows from the written put option holders"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 260-10-45-35, in-the-money written put options (exercise price $50 > market price $40) are dilutive. The reverse treasury stock method assumes shares are issued at market to fund the repurchase: 20,000 x $50 / $40 = 25,000 shares issued, producing 5,000 incremental shares (25,000 − 20,000).
Wrong (A): While the incremental share count is correct, omitting the reverse treasury stock method makes this incomplete.
Wrong (B): The puts are in the money ($50 > $40), so they are included, not excluded.
Wrong (D): Written puts obligate the company to buy shares at a price above market — this is dilutive, not accretive.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10054;

