-- Q11541: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both frameworks require recognition because 60% exceeds the probability threshold under both standards","Neither framework recognizes a liability because 60% probability is insufficient under both standards","IFRS requires a provision at 60% probability; U.S. GAAP requires only disclosure since 60% is below probable","U.S. GAAP requires recognition at 60% probability while IFRS requires only disclosure at that threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IAS 37.14, probable means more likely than not (>50%), so 60% triggers provision recognition of $5,200,000. Under ASC 450-20-25, probable is interpreted as approximately 75% or higher. At 60%, the loss is reasonably possible — requiring disclosure but not accrual.
Wrong (A): U.S. GAAP''s probable threshold (~75%) is not met at 60%, so only IFRS requires recognition.
Wrong (B): IFRS recognizes a provision at 60% since it exceeds the more-likely-than-not threshold.
Wrong (D): This reverses the frameworks — IFRS has the lower threshold (>50%), not U.S. GAAP.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11541;

-- Q11542: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["NRV and fair value produce the same $275,000 result because both reflect current market conditions","NRV is $250,000 (selling price minus completion and selling costs); fair value is $275,000 (the orderly transaction price)","NRV is $290,000 (the unadjusted selling price); fair value is $320,000 (the original historical cost)","Both measurements equal $290,000 because the estimated selling price is the only relevant measurement input"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 330-10-35, NRV equals selling price ($290,000) minus costs to complete ($15,000) and costs to sell ($25,000), totaling $250,000. Under ASC 820, fair value is the exit price in an orderly transaction — $275,000. NRV is entity-specific while fair value is market-based.
Wrong (A): NRV ($250,000) and fair value ($275,000) differ because NRV deducts entity-specific costs that fair value does not.
Wrong (C): NRV is not the unadjusted selling price, and fair value is not historical cost — both are current measures.
Wrong (D): NRV requires deducting completion and selling costs from the selling price, yielding $250,000.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11542;

-- Q11543: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Historical cost — the original exchange price at acquisition date","Current replacement cost — the amount to acquire an equivalent asset today","Net realizable value — selling price less completion and disposal costs","Present value of future cash flows — the discounted amount of expected inflows or outflows"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under SFAC No. 5 and SFAC No. 8, present value measures assets or liabilities at the discounted amount of future net cash flows. This attribute is used for long-term receivables, lease liabilities (ASC 842), and pension obligations (ASC 715), incorporating the time value of money.
Wrong (A): Historical cost measures the exchange price at acquisition without discounting future cash flows.
Wrong (B): Current replacement cost reflects what it would cost to acquire an equivalent asset today, not future cash flows.
Wrong (C): Net realizable value is the estimated selling price less costs, not a discounted future cash flow measure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11543;

-- Q11594: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Failure to include a required statement of cash flows with the tax-basis statements","Failure to disclose the basis of accounting used and how it differs from GAAP","Failure to present comparative financial statements for at least two periods","Failure to include earnings per share data on the face of the income statement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA standards and ASC 105-10-05, financial statements prepared using a special purpose framework must include a note disclosing the basis of accounting and summarizing significant differences from GAAP. Without this, users cannot assess how the statements differ from GAAP-basis reporting.
Wrong (A): While a cash flow statement may be useful, the omitted basis-of-accounting note is the more fundamental deficiency for SPF statements.
Wrong (C): Comparative statements are not the primary deficiency — the missing basis disclosure is far more significant.
Wrong (D): Earnings per share is a GAAP requirement (ASC 260) that does not apply to tax-basis financial statements.$EXPL$
