-- Q11825: Not-for-Profit Accounting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recognize $200,000 as contribution revenue classified with donor restrictions on use","Recognize $200,000 as contribution revenue classified without any donor restrictions","Record a $200,000 refundable advance liability until the funds are actually received","Do not recognize any amount — an intention to give is not an unconditional promise"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 958-605-25-2, contribution revenue is recognized only when an unconditional promise to give is made. A mere intention or expression of willingness does not constitute a legally enforceable promise and creates no recognizable asset for the NFP.
Wrong (A): No revenue can be recognized because no unconditional promise exists — the statement is only an expression of intent.
Wrong (B): Same issue — without an unconditional promise, no contribution revenue of any type is recognized.
Wrong (C): A refundable advance applies when cash is received with conditions; here, no cash or binding promise was made.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11825;

-- Q11831: Not-for-Profit Accounting [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Contribution revenue with donor restrictions because the funds have stipulated purposes","Contribution revenue without donor restrictions because the funds were received unconditionally","An agency liability, net of the $15,000 processing fee earned by Sycamore","A refundable advance liability until the funds are distributed to the three charities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 958-605-25-24 through 25-27, an NFP receiving assets with instructions to transfer them to specified beneficiaries, without variance power, acts as an agent. Sycamore has no discretion, and the foundation retains recall rights. The $15,000 fee (2% x $750,000) is agency fee revenue; the remaining $735,000 is a liability.
Wrong (A): Sycamore lacks variance power and discretion — it is an agent, not a donee, so no contribution revenue is recognized.
Wrong (B): The funds are not Sycamore''s revenue; they must be passed through to specified beneficiaries.
Wrong (D): A refundable advance implies Sycamore will eventually earn the revenue; as an agent, it never does.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11831;

-- Q11836: Not-for-Profit Accounting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$120,000 contribution revenue with $28,000 reported as a fundraising expense","$85,000 contribution revenue, $35,000 exchange revenue, and $28,000 cost of direct benefits to donors","$120,000 special event revenue with $28,000 netted against it as a direct offset","$85,000 contribution revenue with $28,000 reported as a fundraising expense"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 958-225-45-5, special events with both contribution and exchange elements are reported separately. The exchange portion equals fair value of benefits ($35,000); the contribution portion is the excess ($120,000 − $35,000 = $85,000). The $28,000 direct costs are reported as cost of direct benefits to donors.
Wrong (A): The full $120,000 is not contribution revenue — $35,000 represents the exchange element for benefits received.
Wrong (C): Netting direct costs against revenue is not permitted; the components must be reported separately.
Wrong (D): This omits the $35,000 exchange revenue component and misclassifies the direct costs as fundraising expense.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11836;

-- Q11958: Debt [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recognize a $100,000 gain and reduce the carrying amount of the liability to $1,250,000","Reduce the carrying amount to $1,100,000 and recognize a gain of $150,000 on restructuring","Compute a new effective interest rate equating the $1,250,000 carrying amount to future payments with no gain","Record the restructured debt at fair value and recognize the difference as a gain in earnings"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 470-60-35-6, when total future cash payments ($1,350,000) exceed the carrying amount ($1,250,000), no gain is recognized. Instead, a new effective interest rate is computed that equates the carrying amount to the present value of modified future payments, and all future payments reduce the liability.
Wrong (A): A gain arises only when future cash flows are less than the carrying amount, which is not the case here.
Wrong (B): The carrying amount is not reduced to principal — the full $1,250,000 remains as the starting balance.
Wrong (D): Fair value extinguishment (ASC 470-50) does not apply to troubled debt restructuring debtor accounting.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11958;

-- Q11968: Debt [medium/L3]
