-- Q12063: Intangible Assets [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Expense both projects totaling $1,800,000 as current-period research and development costs","Continue carrying both as indefinite-life intangible assets until the next annual impairment test","Reclassify both projects to finite-life intangible assets and begin systematic amortization","Reclassify completed Alpha to a finite-life intangible for amortization; write off abandoned Beta as impairment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 350-30-35, IPR&D acquired in a business combination is classified as an indefinite-life intangible until completed or abandoned. Upon completion, Alpha becomes a finite-life asset subject to amortization. Upon abandonment, Beta''s $600,000 carrying amount is written off as an impairment loss.
Wrong (A): Acquired IPR&D is capitalized under ASC 805, not expensed as period R&D — expensing applies only to internally developed R&D.
Wrong (B): Completed and abandoned projects must be reclassified or written off; they cannot remain as indefinite-life assets.
Wrong (C): Abandoned Project Beta cannot be reclassified to finite-life — it must be written off entirely.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12063;

-- Q12064: Intangible Assets [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["10% of original cost, consistent with common tangible asset depreciation conventions","Zero, unless a third party has committed to purchase the asset or an active market exists","Fair value at end of useful life as estimated by management using current market data","The amount recoverable through undiscounted future cash flows from the asset"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 350-30-35-8, the residual value of a finite-life intangible is assumed to be zero unless a third party has committed to purchase it at the end of its useful life, or an active exchange market exists from which residual value can be determined.
Wrong (A): There is no default 10% residual value assumption for intangibles — the GAAP presumption is zero.
Wrong (C): Management estimates of future fair value are not an acceptable basis; a committed buyer or active market is required.
Wrong (D): Undiscounted cash flows relate to impairment testing (ASC 360), not to establishing residual value.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12064;

-- Q12069: Intangible Assets [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$800,000, the original capitalized cost before any amortization or write-down adjustments","$600,000, the current unamortized cost before performing the NRV assessment","$200,000, representing the excess of unamortized cost over the net realizable value","$450,000, because ASC 985-20 requires reporting at the lower of unamortized cost or NRV"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 985-20-35-4, capitalized software costs for external sale are reported at the lower of unamortized cost or NRV at each balance sheet date. NRV ($450,000) is below unamortized cost ($600,000), so the asset is written down to $450,000 with a $150,000 charge to cost of sales.
Wrong (A): Original cost ignores both cumulative amortization and the mandatory NRV ceiling test.
Wrong (B): The unamortized cost ($600,000) exceeds NRV ($450,000), so the lower-of test requires a write-down.
Wrong (C): The $200,000 is the write-down amount, not the reported asset value — the asset is carried at $450,000.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12069;

-- Q12074: Intangible Assets [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$1,250,000 to each reporting unit equally regardless of their relative fair values","The entire $2,500,000 to Unit A because it has the higher relative fair value","$1,675,000 to Unit A and $825,000 to Unit B based on relative fair values of each unit","Goodwill allocation is deferred until the first annual impairment test is performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 350-20-35-40, goodwill is assigned to reporting units expected to benefit from acquisition synergies using relative fair values. Unit A receives 67% x $2,500,000 = $1,675,000 and Unit B receives 33% x $2,500,000 = $825,000.
Wrong (A): Equal allocation ignores the relative benefit analysis required by ASC 350 — units with higher fair values receive more goodwill.
Wrong (B): Both units benefit from the synergies, so goodwill must be allocated to both based on relative fair value.
Wrong (D): Goodwill must be assigned at the acquisition date, not deferred until a future impairment test.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12074;
